From 662bd83e3256be7fe86948e7a6141ccf633c2018 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jetson <jetson@orin-nano.tail52323b.ts.net>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 10:19:06 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] nv_attach: key forwarding on an allowlist; one
 string-aware tokenizer; widths from .reg

A second, scoped review of the forwarding scan (7e5861b9; two reviewers,
each finding reproduced or refuted by another agent; none reachable from
llvmbpf's output today) found it failing open again, because it blocked a
list of opcodes and tokenized loosely:
- Between the key's store and the lookup only instructions that cannot
  write memory may stand (mov, arithmetic, logic, cvt/cvta, setp/selp,
  ld, bra, ...); anything else (st.param through a frame address, wmma,
  any store the pass cannot place) ends forwarding. Stores into other
  slots of the frame still pass.
- One tokenizer for every check: comments become a space, string literals
  are kept whole (a // in a .pragma string), statements split on ';'
  outside strings, guards may be any identifier (@p), the mnemonic is the
  leading run of [A-Za-z0-9_.:] (mov.u32%r7,5 and \r separators).
- The forwarded register's width comes from its .reg declaration (cvt for
  64-bit, mov for 32-bit); without a declaration no forwarding.
- A helper call block the pass replaces must hold only its declarations,
  argument stores, call and return load (else the module is refused, as
  anything else in it would be dropped); a callseq marker counts only at
  a line start (not inside a comment). Offsets are summed with overflow
  checks.
- Definitions are indexed once per body and cached per thread: the rescans
  had made pt_victim's carry run 4.5 s instead of 0.9 s.
Tests: 20 cases, 239 assertions. lat_mmvq's and the four-kernel lat's
generated PTX are unchanged (41b5bf76...; 813a383c, b0cb7b73, e5e3d405), so
the measurements stand; pt_victim's seven tools exact.

